.TH USHER 1 "2026" "usher 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
usher \- record the Wayland window layout and place windows back
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B usher
.I save | restore | doctor | status | toggle | cleanly | ...
.br
.B usher-mgr
.I watch | resume | display-changed
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B usher
seats windows into a steady arrangement on a wlroots/Wayfire session. Instead of
hand-written placement rules it records where windows actually are and puts them
back. Two commands split the job:
.B usher-mgr
is the service the session starts, and
.B usher
is how you ask anything of it. Placement is AGGRESSIVE for a
window after login (or a re-arm), then STEADY: a reopened window just
appears where you are; the opt-in
.I usher/include
anchors keep snapping back even then, and
.I usher/exclude
lists windows never to place.
.PP
An optional
.B usher-indicator
tray icon shows the placement mode and toggles it on a left-click (the
.I indicator
sub-package).
.SH VERBS
.TP
.B watch
The daemon (started at login): record continuously, place on map. Re-running it
reloads a running instance.
.TP
.B capture
Snapshot the current layout into the store now.
.TP
.B restore " [--dry-run] [--only S] [--from SPEC]"
Place the stored layout onto the live session. Without
.B --from
this is the learned knowledge base for the current display profile.
.B --from
restores a stored SNAPSHOT instead:
.I latest
is the newest rolled snapshot (undo a shuffle you just made), while
.IR today ,
.I yesterday
or a
.I YYYY-MM-DD
is that day's FIRST snapshot, the stable "how it looked that morning".
.B --from list
shows what is available.
.TP
.B lock " [--force]"
Lock the seated login session and VERIFY it took, by reading logind's
.I LockedHint
back: `loginctl lock-session` exits 0 whether or not anything is listening, so
its status proves nothing. Non-zero if the session did not actually lock.
usher does not lock anything itself; it asks logind, and whatever the session
registered as its locker does the work.
.SS What usher needs from an app
Nothing, for the part most people want. Three tiers, and each is only worth
reaching for when the one below falls short.
.TP
.B nothing
gets the window back WHERE AND HOW BIG it was. Any window with a title is
captured, keyed and placed, and a single-window app is keyed by its app-id
ALONE, so the slot does not move when the document does. What you give up is
only the starting.
.TP
.B a desktop entry
gets the app STARTED FOR YOU at login. Write one when the app ships none, when
its entry is
.I Terminal=true
(Debian's
.I vim.desktop
is
.IR "Exec=vim %F, Terminal=true" ,
so it describes terminal vim and usher correctly refuses it for a gvim
window), or when its app-id differs from the entry's filename.
.I StartupWMClass
is then the load-bearing line, since the join is on the app-id.
.TP
.B a plugin
is for when IDENTITY is the problem rather than launching. Any one of these
means the tiers above cannot serve you:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
SEVERAL WINDOWS of one app must be told apart. Without a plugin, two or more
windows of the same app fall back to per-TITLE keys, so the slot follows the
document rather than the window. One window is fine; several is what needs a
plugin, and it is the whole reason the chrome plugin exists.
.IP \(bu 2
THE IDENTITY IS NOT IN THE TITLE. chrome reads a SessionID from the browser's
session file, kitty the shell's cwd from
.IR /proc ,
mux the command the window runs. A title that has to be right at the instant
you look is a bootstrap, not an identity.
.IP \(bu 2
THE RELAUNCH DIFFERS PER WINDOW. A desktop entry describes an APP, so the
default starts one invocation per app; a terminal holding a specific remote
session needs its own command.
.IP \(bu 2
IDENTITY IS NOT READY WHEN THE WINDOW MAPS. A plugin can answer
.RI ( "pending" ", " why )
and usher will neither place nor remember the window until it is sure. Nothing
else can express that, and guessing corrupts the slot it was about to aim at.
.IP \(bu 2
THE APP NEEDS LETTING GO CLEANLY.
.I wind_down
is how chrome gets
.I SessionEnded
instead of
.I Crashed
on a reboot.
.RE
.PP
If none of those is true, a desktop entry is the simpler and more portable
answer: no Python, it benefits your launcher and menus too, and it cannot
break when usher's internals move.

.SS The default relaunch
A window whose app no plugin claims is still relaunched at session start, from
the freedesktop registry: the app-id is joined to a
.I .desktop
entry by filename and then by
.IR StartupWMClass ,
its
.I Exec=
is taken, and the field codes are stripped so the app opens with no document.
.B The only command usher ever runs is an Exec= out of a desktop file it read,
never a stored string and never a
.I /proc
cmdline, which cannot be replayed safely: a browser rewrites its own argv, a
terminal's cmdline carries flags usher itself injected, and a one-off file
argument would reopen a file that is gone.
.PP
It refuses rather than guesses when several entries match, when none does, and
when the registry marks the entry
.IR NoDisplay ", " Hidden ", " Terminal=true
or not an
.IR Application ,
which is what stops a tray applet or a D-Bus portal service being started as
though it were an app. A plugin claiming the app overrides it, and
.I USHER_NO_DEFAULT_RELAUNCH=1
disables it. See
.B doctor
for the command it would run per saved window.
.PP
To have usher relaunch something it cannot work out, WRITE A DESKTOP ENTRY in
.IR ~/.local/share/applications .
An app started from a shell with no entry of its own, or whose entry is
.I Terminal=true
(Debian's
.I vim.desktop
is), is still remembered and still placed when you open it; it is simply not
started for you.
.I StartupWMClass
is the key part, since the join is on the app-id.
.I NoDisplay=true
keeps such an entry out of the application menu and usher still honours it:
NoDisplay means "do not show this in menus" and says nothing about launching,
so it is read as a refusal only for an entry you did NOT author.
.I Hidden=true
is a deletion in the spec's own words and is honoured everywhere.
.TP
.B predict " [--out FILE]"
Record what the NEXT restore should produce: every live window with the
workspace, position and size its remembered slot would give it. Writes
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/usher/prediction.json
unless told otherwise. Always exits 0; this records, it does not judge.
.TP
.B verify " [--against FILE]"
Diff the live layout against a prediction and exit non-zero on any window
that is missing or in the wrong place. This is the only end-to-end check that
is not circular:
.B restore --dry-run
compares the store against the screen, so when something teaches the store a
wrong answer the two agree and it reports everything fine. A prediction
written BEFORE the event cannot have been corrupted by it. Record one, reboot,
then verify.
.TP
.B resume
Start/reload in ADOPT mode (adopt the current layout as baseline; do not
restore).
.TP
.B status
The daemon's mode + seconds until it settles.
.TP
.B plugins
List the loaded window plugins (built-in + user).
.TP
.B launch
Respawn saved-but-absent windows now, without starting the daemon.
.TP
.B reload
Pick up new code in a running daemon and change NOTHING else: no restore, no
re-baselining of the remembered layout, no relaunch. This is what a deploy
wants;
.B resume
looks right for it and is not, because it captures.
.TP
.B wind-down
Bring the session to a clean stop, for a power menu to call before it reboots:
capture while the layout is still true, stop the watcher so nothing overwrites
that capture, then ask each app to exit cleanly under a bounded deadline. Does
not reboot or log out. Always exits 0.
.TP
.B display-changed
For a display-change hook. Re-places against the profile for the new monitor
set, and does nothing at all when the set has not actually changed.
.TP
.B aggressive | settle | toggle
Re-arm aggressive placement, force steady now, or flip between them.
.TP
.B stop
Stop the running daemon cleanly.
.TP
.B exclude | include
Show the never-place and anchor rules as loaded, with any parse errors.
.TP
.B doctor
Report what usher is and is not doing: the store (entries per app, the display
profile, schema), the plugin registry, the cross-tool contracts, and per window
what would be relaunched or placed and WHY not. Exit 1 on a contract breach;
exit 2 when it could not see the live session at all (no
.BR WAYFIRE_SOCKET ,
no pywayfire, or no compositor answering), in which case the window sections
are SKIPPED rather than answered from nothing and the report says so loudly.
A non-interactive
.BR ssh (1)
carries none of the session environment, which is the usual way to meet it.
.TP
.B selftest
Offline unit checks (no compositor).
.SH DISPLAY PROFILES
A placement only means anything on the monitors it was learned on, so the
knowledge base is keyed by the CONNECTED DISPLAY SET: docked and undocked
remember separate layouts rather than overwriting one another, and a window
whose remembered output is not attached is reported
.B UNPLACEABLE
rather than skipped in silence.
.PP
.BR hwdp (1)
supplies the profile id when it is installed (a soft dependency). Without it
the id is derived from the outputs the last snapshot saw, and failing that
everything shares one
.I default
profile, which is the historical behaviour. Override with
.BR USHER_PROFILE .
A pre-profile store is adopted into the current profile once, so no learned
placement is lost on upgrade.
.SH IDENTITY AND PLUGINS
A window is matched to its saved slot by (app_id, key). App-SPECIFIC identity
and respawn live in PLUGINS. Three ship built in, and none keys on what the
window is currently SHOWING: a window that can display many things would
otherwise lose its place whenever you switched:
.TP
.B chrome
keys a window by its SessionID, read from the browser's own session file. That
id is fresh every time Chrome restarts, so a window is followed across the
boundary by the TABS it came back with, joining the previous session file to
the new one. A window Chrome has not recorded (an incognito or guest window
never is) has no identity here and is not remembered at all. Starts the
browser per profile, with
.BR --restore-last-session ,
when the last session had Chrome windows and none is running.
.TP
.B mux
keys a terminal by the COMMAND it runs
.RB ( term:resume ", or " term:latch\ <host>:<session> ),
so switching sessions inside a window does not forfeit its place. Relaunch
replays that command: a
.B mux latch
is reproduced exactly, and anything else was a local mux, for which
.B mux resume
rebuilds the whole recorded set rather than the one session a titlebar happened
to name (a soft dependency: absent \fBmux\fR degrades to a no-op).
.TP
.B kitty
keys any other kitty terminal by its shell's working directory (from /proc) and
respawns it as a shell there.
.PP
A user adds a plugin as a
.I *.py
file in
.I ~/.config/usher/plugins/
defining a top-level
.B PLUGIN
object: see the shipped
.I share/plugins/example.py .
Each plugin claims an app's windows
.RB ( owns )
and may implement
.BR resolve ", " relaunch_command ", "
.BR relaunch_missing ", and " wind_down ,
each taking a normalized view
.RB ( v["app"] ", " v["title"] ", " v["pid"] ", " v["id"] ).
.I v["id"]
is the compositor's view id, stable for exactly one window's lifetime and
.I None
for a window replayed from the store, so it is what to cache a hard-won
identity against.
.PP
.B resolve
answers in three ways and the third is the point:
.IR ("ready", " key)" ,
.IR ("pending", " why)" ,
.IR ("never", " why)" .
usher neither places nor remembers a window that is not ready, so a plugin
that cannot yet say WHICH window it is must say that rather than guess: a
guessed key is written to the store and then placed against, which is worse
than waiting. The reason is what
.B doctor
prints for an unmatched window, so the plugin that knows supplies it. The
2-tuple keeps plugins free of any usher import.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/usher/{exclude,include}
never-place rules and steady-state anchors.
.TP
.I ~/.config/usher/plugins/*.py
user window plugins.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/usher/
the layout store (current snapshot, history, knowledge base).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mux (1)
